  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-94025",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4)-4+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={e}^{-\\pi }$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q{e}^{-\\pi } & ={e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ Q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi },$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2{e}^{-2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{-2x}(-P\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=6{e}^{-\\pi }$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2Q{e}^{-\\pi }-P{e}^{-\\pi } & =6{e}^{-\\pi } \\\\ -2Q-P & =6 \\\\ -2-P & =6 \\\\ P & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=-8$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{-2x}(-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
